body:
To illustrate my doubt, what happens if you do this?

```js
    const img = new Image();
    img.src = "4000x4000.jpg";
    img.decode(100, 100).then(() => {
        document.querySelector("#small-image").src = img.src;
    });
    img.decode(200, 200).then(() => {
        document.querySelector("#medium-image").src = img.src;
    });
    document.querySelector("#large-image").src = img.src;
```

Because images are keyed by URL, it's unclear how the above is expected to behave.

> Can you just key the decode requests by element and requested size?

So this would decode the image twice?
```js
    const img = new Image();
    img.src = "4000x4000.jpg";
    img.decode(100, 100);
    document.body.append(img);
    document.querySelector("#large-image").src = img.src;
```

I think that keying by a URL of some kind or some other declarative form expressed in HTML would match the rest of the platform better than relying purely on JS for this.

> To illustrate my doubt, what happens if you do this?
> 
>     const img = new Image();
>     img.src = "4000x4000.jpg";
>     img.decode(100, 100).then(() => {
>         document.querySelector("#small-image").src = img.src;
>     });
>     img.decode(200, 200).then(() => {
>         document.querySelector("#medium-image").src = img.src;
>     });
>     document.querySelector("#large-image").src = img.src;
> Because images are keyed by URL, it's unclear how the above is expected to behave.

A decoded frame can be reused only if `max_dimension(decoded_frame) >= max_dimension(requested_frame)`.  So in your example the frame will be decoded two times through decode() and one time through display. This is because you made the wrong choice. You decoded from the smallest size to the largest size. But if you reverse the order:
```
    const img = new Image();
    img.src = "4000x4000.jpg";
    img.decode().then(() => {
        document.querySelector("#large-image").src = img.src;
    });
    img.decode(200, 200).then(() => {
        document.querySelector("#medium-image").src = img.src;
    });
    document.querySelector("#small-image").src = img.src;
```
Then the frame will be decoded only once which is with its natural size { 4000, 4000 }. The second `decode(200, 200)` will use the result of the previous `decode()`. At display time no extra decoding will be done.

This is the best way to write your example:
```
    const img = new Image();
    img.src = "4000x4000.jpg";
    img.decode().then(() => {
        document.querySelector("#small-image").src = img.src;
        document.querySelector("#medium-image").src = img.src;
        document.querySelector("#large-image").src = img.src;
    });
```
I think the key factor with image decode() and image display is to never affect resolution. So { 4000, 4000 } frame is suitable for decode() and displaying small and medium images but not the other way around.

My proposal will be good for simple scenarios like this:
```
    const img = new Image();
    img.src = "4000x4000.jpg";
    img.decode(100, 100).then(() => {
        document.querySelector("#small-image").src = img.src;
    });
```

> OK I see. That makes sense, though I still wish this didn't _have_ to depend on script. There are pure-HTML use cases for this I think. Perhaps it can be a boolean attribute on `<img>` or some such to make it decode using the specified width/height?

Do you mean something like this?
```
<script>
    const img = new Image();
    img.width = 100;
    img.height = 100;
    img.src = "4000x4000.jpg";
    img.decode().then(() => { // img will be decoded for size { 100, 100 }
        document.querySelector("#small-image").src = img.src;
    })
<script>
```
Yes this may be another way to control the decoding size.

So one thing that might be tricky is that for canvas / etc you care about image pixels (as in, the bitmap pixel count). But that seems very footgunny for HTML use cases:

```
    img.decode(100, 100).then(() => { // img will be decoded for size { 100, 100 }
        document.querySelector("#small-image").src = img.src;
    })
```

But that will in fact do a useless decode on a hidpi screen if those are image pixels. Maybe it should take CSS pixels? But then canvas use cases need to multiply by devicePixelRatio, and you get fractional pixels potentially...
